PROJECT 1: IT TROUBLESHOOTING & TICKET MANAGEMENT

Portfolio Project Report

Environment: Windows 11 host, VirtualBox, Ubuntu Server, and Kali Linux
Project type: Practical home-lab simulation
Focus areas: IT support, troubleshooting methodology, Linux administration, networking, service monitoring, and incident documentation

1. Project Overview

This project demonstrates a structured approach to investigating and documenting common IT support incidents in a controlled virtual lab environment.

The objective was to practise gathering evidence, performing diagnostic tests, identifying what the evidence supported, documenting outcomes, and verifying recovery where applicable.

Six incident tickets were developed to simulate help-desk scenarios.

2. Lab Environment

Windows 11 host operating system

Oracle VirtualBox virtualization platform

Ubuntu Server virtual machine

Kali Linux virtual machine

Apache HTTP Server

Linux networking, service-management, logging, and storage utilities

The work was performed in a home lab. The tickets represent simulated incidents, not production support cases.

3. Troubleshooting Methodology

The following workflow was used:

Record the reported symptoms and define the scope.

Inspect the relevant system or service.

Run appropriate diagnostic commands.

Capture and review the results.

Compare observations with expected behaviour.

Apply corrective action only when justified.

Verify the outcome.

Document the findings, limitations, and final status.

A baseline file and a VirtualBox snapshot were also created to support controlled testing and recovery.

4. Incident Summary

HD-001 — Internet Connectivity Investigation

Tools: ping, nslookup, PowerShell Test-NetConnection, and a browser.

Connectivity, DNS resolution, TCP connectivity to port 443, and browser access tests succeeded during testing.

Outcome: The reported issue was not reproduced. Its original cause was not established.

HD-002 — Ubuntu Server Password Recovery

Tools: Ubuntu recovery mode, root shell, and account verification commands.

Recovery access was obtained, the cyberadmin password was reset, and normal login was subsequently verified.

Outcome: Access was restored in the lab and post-recovery verification was performed.

HD-003 — Network Interface Recovery

Tools: ip -br addr, ip route, and ping.

A controlled test temporarily disabled the enp0s3 interface. The interface was restored, and connectivity checks succeeded, including a ping to 1.1.1.1 and punchng.com.

Outcome: Network connectivity was restored and verified in the lab.

HD-004 — Web Service Availability

Tools: systemctl, ss, and curl.

Apache was active, port 80 was listening, and HTTP requests succeeded locally, through the server's 10.10.10.10 address, and from Kali Linux. The requests returned HTTP 200 OK.

Outcome: The reported web-access problem was not reproduced during testing.

HD-005 — Disk Space Investigation

Tools: df -h, df -i, and du.

The root filesystem was approximately 58% utilized, with about 4.0 GB available. Inode utilization was approximately 1% in the recorded check. The /var directory used approximately 814 MB, including 359 MB in /var/lib, 322 MB in /var/cache, and 123 MB in /var/log.

Outcome: No immediate storage-capacity emergency was indicated. No files were deleted.

HD-006 — Application and Service Health

Tools: systemctl, journalctl, tail, apache2ctl configtest, and curl.

Apache was active and enabled. The configuration test returned Syntax OK, and requests to 127.0.0.1 and 127.0.1.1 returned HTTP 200 OK. A hostname-related warning was observed.

Outcome: No Apache outage was reproduced. The warning was documented without changing configuration.

5. Skills Demonstrated

Structured incident investigation and ticket documentation

Basic network connectivity and DNS troubleshooting

Linux account recovery and access verification

Network interface and routing-table inspection

HTTP service and listening-port verification

Linux filesystem capacity and inode checks

Service status, journal, and error-log analysis

Configuration validation and post-test verification

Evidence capture and safe change practices

6. Key Lessons Learned

Test connectivity in layers rather than assuming the cause.

A running service does not automatically prove that every client can access it.

Successful diagnostic tests should be recorded accurately without claiming an unverified fix.

Disk usage should be measured before deleting files or clearing caches.

Service logs and configuration tests provide useful evidence when diagnosing application issues.

Recovery safeguards and verification reduce risk during controlled troubleshooting.

7. Limitations and Scope

This was a controlled home-lab project using virtual machines. It does not represent production-system administration or enterprise service-desk employment.

Several simulated reports could not be reproduced by the tests performed. Their original causes therefore remain undetermined.

8. Evidence and Supporting Documentation

Supporting materials are organized by ticket under the project's evidence directory. These include terminal screenshots, diagnostic outputs, and ticket records.

The supporting evidence should be reviewed alongside each ticket to verify the commands run and the outcomes recorded.

9. Conclusion

This project provided practical experience in following a repeatable troubleshooting process, using windows and Linux diagnostic tools, validating service behaviour, protecting system stability, and documenting evidence-based outcomes.

It forms the first project in a broader IT support portfolio intended to demonstrate practical readiness for entry-level help-desk and technical-support opportunities.